  "answers": {
    "traditional_rag": "**CFTR → dysbiosis** – *direct animal evidence*  \n- Whole‑body and tissue‑specific Cftr knockout mice develop altered gut communities: loss of Cftr in epithelial cells shifts large‑intestinal microbiota, while immune‑cell loss drives small‑intestinal dysbiosis (reduced α‑diversity, ↑Proteobacteria, Enterobacteriaceae, Clostridia) [PMID: 36289287] (mouse); intestinal‑specific Cftr KO alone recapitulates these taxonomic changes [PMID: 37546931] (mouse).  \n\n**Dysbiosis → intestinal inflammation** – *direct animal evidence*  \n- Cftr‑deficient mice with dysbiotic profiles show elevated fecal calprotectin and mucosal inflammation; antibiotic depletion of the dysbiotic flora restores intestinal barrier function and lowers peri‑portal liver inflammation, indicating the microbiota is necessary for the inflammatory phenotype [PMID: 38554847] (mouse).  \n\n**CFTR → dysbiosis → inflammation** – *integrated mechanistic chain*  \n- In iCftr KO mice, dysbiosis (β‑diversity shifts, ↑Proteobacteria, ↑Clostridia) is sufficient to raise calprotectin, and gut‑corrected CFTR mice (CFTR‑KO‑GC) lack both dysbiosis and inflammation, demonstrating that defective CFTR creates a pathogenic microbiota that drives inflammation [PMID: 37546931] (mouse)."
  },
